WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
Thanks to brilliant.org for sponsoring this video

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:05.500
Fantasy is filled with incredible flying creatures

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.000
from the dragons of Game of Thrones
to the hippogriffs of Harry Potter.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
They're unlike anything we see in the natural world in both form and size.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.000
Thank God.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.500
But being both a serious geek and a scientist,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.529
I can't help but wonder:
Could these creatures really exist?

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:21.700
Does the physics add up that they could fly?

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.600
To answer this question in this video

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.600
I'm going to apply real physics and biology to fantasy creatures

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.200
by looking at extant and extinct species of bats and birds

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.500
and by talking to experts on how those creatures fly.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:39.600
But to begin with, we need to know a bit more about flying fantasy creatures,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.000
and while I know a few things about fantasy,

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.900
I happen to know some people who are much more qualified to talk about it.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.400
My name is Mark Hulmes

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.360
I call myself a professional dungeon master, which sounds very very -

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:52.800
full of myself.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.800
I run a Dungeons &amp; Dragons campaign called High Rollers.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.000
We have our own Twitch channel,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.800
but we're also partnered with the Yogscast that we stream on every Sunday at 5 p.m. GMT

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.600
and we've been doing it for two years now.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.400
My name is Mark Turpin or Turps from the Yogscast.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.600
I'm the CEO here.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.800
The way I met the guys and got my job and all of that is

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.500
I've been playing World of Warcraft now for about 15 years.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.929
It's where in essence the Yogscast was formed and

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.800
it's where I spend the vast majority of my time.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.500
And I've been playing and talking about World of Warcraft for -

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.200
probably in time most of my life.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.100
So it's fair to say then that these guys know their stuff.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.659
I asked them to go through what kind of flying creatures exist in fantasy and got a

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.400
comprehensive list.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.800
Yeah, there's a lot of them.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.600
Griffins,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.300
dragons,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.810
the Horde had wyverns,

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.800
manticores, chimeras,

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:43.800
bone drakes,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.700
pegasus,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.500
hippogriffs,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.800
beholders,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.400
horses can fly in Warcraft.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.600
Sometimes they give them wings and
they're like Pegasus,

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.800
sometimes they just have sparkly hooves.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.300
There's a creature called a darkmantle,
which is like an umbrella

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.500
that's like evil and is like an octopus umbrella that like falls down from the ceiling.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.000
It's very very weird. Some of the old D&amp;D stuff is very very strange.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.400
There's a piloted sky golem,
which is basically like an Iron Man suit.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.700
You just kind of put it on and - whoosh! - off you go.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:18.400
So you have creatures that fly via magic
like the beholder who's a big bag of floating eyes,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.600
winged creatures or bat winged creatures.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.000
And I think like winged creatures you're talking about things like griffins, pegasi

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.899
and then the bat wings is for creatures like

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.000
chimeras, manticores, dragons, devils, that kind of stuff.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.600
And I think they fall into those two categories.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.000
So necessarily disregarding those creatures which fly exclusively through magic,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.000
we're left with mythical creatures which fly in a way comparable to real animals.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.000
To begin with, let's focus on those creatures with bird wings.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.800
But, it turns out there's something of a problem with applying physics to bird flight.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.000
We don't really know how it works.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.369
So we've got a good idea of how birds and insects and planes fly, how they generate lift,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.800
but it's a very complex interaction of forces.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.500
And yeah, we still don't have it all quite nailed down.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.800
That's Dr Alex Evans who just completed his PhD in bird flight.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.400
My PhD is on the biomechanics and energetics of bird flight.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.600
I work with birds, mostly small parrots.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.459
We have a wind tunnel at the university,

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:21.300
so we're able to fly them at a variety of speeds

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.500
and whilst they're flying we can measure various mechanical and metabolic aspects of their flight

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.800
and we can also film the birds flying and generate kinematic analysis

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.400
and 3D model reconstructions of what the wings are doing in flight.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.820
So birds don't all just fly in one uniform way.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.400
There's a variety of different styles of flights

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.000
depending on how big the bird is.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.600
Bigger birds tend to have a slower wing beat

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.600
and they generally make use of

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:51.000
energy efficient methods of flying such as soaring

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.000
and they're able to fly- sustain flight for a very long time with little energy.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.600
Whereas other birds, much smaller birds,
tend to use flapping flight a lot more.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.600
But even they don't just flap all the time.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.600
They might have a bounding flight where
they're flapping for a certain amount of time

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.500
and then they're gliding for a certain amount of time.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.400
So it varies a lot depending on how fast the bird's going,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.849
how big they are,

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:14.500
generally what they're using their flight for,
whether it's hunting or escaping?

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.319
So to apply physics to our flying mythical creatures,

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.200
we have to be more specific
in how we expect them to fly.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.400
Looking at the creatures in World of Warcraft and D&amp;D,

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:28.300
most of the wings seem to be similar to those of medium to large raptors

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.600
like eagles and hawks,
which fly using a soaring or

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.400
intermittent flapping gate depending on what they're doing.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.000
Given this information, we can now ask a bunch of questions.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.800
Perhaps the most basic of which is:

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.400
Based on the species we know fly in this way,

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.800
are the wings depicted on fantasy creatures actually

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.800
large enough to carry them aloft?

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.200
To try and answer that question I use this paper published in 1989 by Mendelssohn et al.,

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.000
which measured various characteristics of a large number of African raptors.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.400
Using data from this paper,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.300
if you plot the mass of these species against their respective wing spans,

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.600
you get this graph.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.020
As the mass of the bird increases,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.400
so too does its wingspan, as we'd expect.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.300
If you could fit a line to this data,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:09.800
you could extrapolate and ask

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:12.300
for a very heavy mythical creature like a griffin,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.600
how big would its wings need to be?

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.200
However, this data isn't linear.
You can't fit a simple straight line to it.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:24.000
So my idea of a simple extrapolation to much heavier animals didn't work.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.000
Fortunately, Dr Evans had a much better suggestion.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:32.980
Because an alternative way of measuring wings to wingspan is wing area.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.040
And wrapped up in the wing area is information about how a bird flies in just two numbers.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.000
Firstly, if you divide the wing area of a bird
by its body mass

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.900
you get a quantity known as wing loading.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:51.480
This is effectively the amount of weight that each square centimeter of the wing has to support in flight.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.200
That's the first number.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.800
The second number is the aspect ratio of the wing,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.400
that is how long the wing is versus how wide it is.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:05.500
So an albatross has very long wings with a relatively low area and so has a high aspect ratio,

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.279
while a puffin on the other hand has adorable short wings,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.000
but they're wide so has a low aspect ratio.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.700
If you scatter plot these two quantities, the wing loading and the aspect ratio for a variety of bird species,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.000
then you get a really interesting graph.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.900
Birds which soar and don't really flap their wings

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.559
like swifts and albatrosses are up here at the top.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.350
Compare that to birds which can only just about fly
like puffins, which are way over here on the right.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:38.800
Populating this graph with lots of species you'll see that the African birds of prey in the paper are all clustered around one area.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.500
That means two things.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:44.500
Firstly, that their wings all share roughly the same wing loading.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.100
If you plot the wing area of these species against their mass,

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:49.000
it's a lovely straight line.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.900
That means that we can extrapolate it neatly to a giant mythical animal

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.800
and get a rough prediction of how much wing area
it would need to fly like an eagle.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.300
Secondly, the birds all share approximately the same wing aspect ratio.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.900
And because of this we can convert the predicted wing area to a predicted wingspan.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.500
So our wing area graph allows us to predict a wing area for any mass of animal that we want

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:16.600
and we can then convert that wing area to a wing span by assuming an aspect ratio

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.500
informed by the data in the paper.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.400
What we find is that for an animal flying like a raptor

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.800
and weighing a ton

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.400
- the weight of a large horse and so what I approximated the way every creature like a griffin would be -

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.500
its wingspan would need to be just under 20 meters.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.000
For context, that's nearly double the wingspan of the classic Cessna 172 light aircraft.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.600
For a giant creature like a griffin to fly,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.400
it would need to have huge wings.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.800
Much bigger than it's depicted to have in for example World of Warcraft.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.700
What's cool about this calculation though

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:53.200
is that you can calculate a wingspan for a flying creature flying like a raptor with any mass,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.969
including the mass of a human

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.800
- I weigh about 90 kilograms.
- 90 kilos.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.000
So you would have a wingspan just shy of
6 meters if you were a bird.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.000
- So about three meters either way?
- Yeah.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.800
Which incidentally is the wingspan of one of the very largest birds in the fossil record,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.600
the condor-like Argentavis.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.800
Which must have been absolutely terrifying.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.900
However, there is a huge problem with this analysis.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:21.100
It's not just because in adding wings to a body,
you're increasing the mass of the body,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.000
and so increasing the size of the wings needed,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.400
and so increasing the mass of the body and so on.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.000
It's to do with how the wings would be powered and connected.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.159
The problem with most mythical creatures, 
such as dragons and

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.828
other large flying beasts that also tend to have four limbs as well as wings,

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.399
is the musculature just doesn't quite make sense.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:44.899
So for birds to fly,

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.888
they rely a lot on the pectoralis muscles.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.300
So in the birds I work with, small parrots,

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.400
they make up 75% of their total muscle mass

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.400
and about 15% of their total body mass.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.979
And so if you have say a hippogriff,

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.500
it's got four legs and it's pecs muscles are being used, they're already attached to some limbs.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:06.500
So where these muscles that

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:10.500
power the giant wings on their back come from,
I don't know.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.800
How they would attach is beyond me.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.000
So for a creature depicted to have both
wings and front legs,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.000
like hippogriffs or griffins,

00:09:19.069 --> 00:09:21.800
musculature just doesn't make any sense.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:24.800
With front legs there are no muscles left
to power the wings.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.600
So the feather creatures we've seen have wings which are too small

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.200
and have no muscles to power them.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.700
So unfortunately based on known biology
they couldn't exist.

00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:38.828
Dragons and dragonlike creatures

00:09:38.829 --> 00:09:41.229
however are a very different story.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.500
Hulmes was pessimistic about whether
anything like them could really exist.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.759
That stuff like dragons, like I mean you look at this and like the bulk of it,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.000
I don't think that unless...

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.300
I don't know maybe the strength, its
strength would be enough to make it fly?

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:55.490
I don't know.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.269
I struggle with that one, but I would say
generally no, I don't think so.

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:00.969
But just wait, let's apply some science.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.000
The closest parallel to dragon wings in biology are those of megabats.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:11.000
You might think of bats as being small insectivorous animals, probably not much bigger than your hand,

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.500
but they get really big.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.200
These are Livingstone's fruit bats,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.800
some of the largest bats in the world,

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.900
kept in captivity at Durrell,

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.600
a conservation charity on the island of Jersey

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:29.079
Livingstone's are about 1.4 meter wingspan
with about 700 to 800 grams body mass,

00:10:29.079 --> 00:10:30.800
so that puts them up there pretty high.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.100
The largest is - God, my Latin pronunciation is terrible -

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.180
but it's the golden-crowned flying fox from the Philippines

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.320
and it can get up to I think it's about 1.8 meter wingspan and

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.800
and then they say a 1.6 kilo bat.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.000
So that's a pretty hefty bat.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.100
That's Ed Bell, a keeper of mammals at Durrell,
who works with the bats.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:55.600
I asked him how bat flight was different to bird flight.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.300
It comes down to the anatomy of bats.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:58.800
Effectively they're flying with their hands.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.400
Chiroptera does come from the - I think the really bad breakdown is Greek for hand wing.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.140
So you've got basically the bones of our hand

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:07.980
but imagine what makes up our palm
were mushed up under the thumb,

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.260
so that big hook they've got is a lovely big thumb.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.320
The second and third digit, so your index and your middle finger,

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.900
they create what would be like the leading edge of a wing,

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.820
so if you're thinking of like the Batman symbol, that will be the bit that goes all the way out right to the edge.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.600
And the two points that kind of come down and create those lovely kind of big arcs,

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.860
that will be their fourth and fifth digits.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:28.400
And each of those has got a nice big

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.300
really clever piece of skin attached between it.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.100
Really cool that she's covered in like micro hairs

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:34.460
'cause they're mammals just like us.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.300
They have got loads of hairs all over their bodies.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.200
Some studies are trying to show they can detect ever so slightly changes

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.200
in like barometric pressure, airspeed, wind speed as they're flying

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.520
and that creates really like micro alterations in the surface of the wing.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:50.800
So when you look at a bat's wing, you've got a perfect complete surface area,

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.280
there is no gaps in it

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:55.440
Now that differs from birds in the sense
that feathers obviously,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.540
they're growing out of the bones
at the ends of the bird's wing.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.080
And they're controllable in a sense, they can shift and change a little bit,

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.600
but they're not as dexterous as a bat's wing.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:08.240
So whereas birds can actually get
quite a lot of nice lightweight lift,

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.460
they can be - well certain species can be incredibly agile,

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:13.920
bats have got a little bit of an edge in terms of agility,

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.040
because their fingers can control every single part of the wing membrane.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.200
They can collapse, they can crunch
and they can allow that kind of

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.040
instant turn and movement.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:25.940
What I wanted to do for the bats was the same calculation we did earlier for birds,

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:30.340
extrapolating from current species
to some huge mythical animal.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:34.320
But Ed mentioned something really interesting about bat flight specifically

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.880
that makes such extrapolation impossible.

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:41.090
There's a limit to how much muscle and kind of like weight to muscle ratio and also

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:45.000
flapping motions that a bat can create when she lifts its way off the ground.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.800
Exponentially, they wouldn't be able to kind of have more muscle and flap harder,

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.000
so there is like a cap on how much a bat could weigh

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:53.600
and they estimate about 1.6.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.660
At the minute will probably be the heaviest

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.439
and as far as the fossil record goes as well

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.000
that's probably the biggest we've ever seen.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.460
I've got a paper I can send you to have a quick flick through.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:04.680
That was this paper from Norberg and Norberg,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.520
which is a fascinating if dense read.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.020
Basically the authors looked on
how as bats get larger and larger,

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:15.000
the wingbeat frequency required
to take flight decreases.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:19.300
So bigger bats don't have to flap their wings as quickly as small bats to take off.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.960
But at the same time,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.540
the fastest that a bat can beat its wings also decreases

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.260
as the bat gets bigger.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:28.680
Their wings just get too big to flap quickly.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:35.100
And interestingly, the maximum wingbeat frequency possible decreases faster with size

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.260
than the minimum wingbeat frequency
required for flight.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.100
So that means there's a theoretical limit

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:42.820
on how big a creature can be with bat wings

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.760
and still be able to fly.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.840
A limit that we've probably already hit

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:49.220
in the Philippine golden-crowned flying fox.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:00.980
Oh my god, I forgot how big it was.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.820
This kind of kills the discussion of dragons dead.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.500
Do you think it's too soon to make that joke?

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:15.100
But if we assume that mythical creatures have some different muscle composition

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.180
or wings with slightly different properties,

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:19.080
then maybe we could paper over this

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:24.900
So I asked Ed which mythical creatures he thought could exist based on some modified bat biology.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:26.060
Wyverns could work.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.780
They've got like the same kind of body structure in a way,

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:30.080
maybe a quite large tail.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:34.440
I think mass size - that when they start to get as big as dragons are depicted,

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:39.060
I just don't know how that could ever lift off.

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:40.140
Smaller dragons maybe.

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:45.080
If you had like baby dragons or all the dragons from Game of Thrones before they got too big,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.060
I could see them working.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:50.140
But when they get to like full size
and you can start riding them,

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.100
I think physics might be on the other side

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:54.660
and that's where magic comes into it so

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:56.020
completely fine

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:59.440
Relying heavily on this excuse of magic then,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.600
Hulmes and I decided to do a bit of backyard maths

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.280
on the ancient red dragon model he had.

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:05.660
It doesn't have bird wings,

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:08.960
but we thought it might be fun to see
what gate it might have.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.600
Measuring its wings to the best of our abilities

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.300
and trawling various D&amp;D resources for its weight,

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:17.400
we estimated the dragon's wing loading and aspect ratio.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:19.880
And comparing them to the bird species from before,

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:21.840
it places on the graph here.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.780
Very similar to condors.

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:26.040
So not flying like a megabat at all.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:27.920
Instead it would soar

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.780
and probably need help in taking off,

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:30.880
like jumping off a cliff

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.500
So apparently it would be about the same as a condor.

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.180
There you go, look at that!

00:15:35.180 --> 00:15:37.600
If it like - if we ignore all the other physical problems.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:41.840
The way I've always done it is like for them to take off is like a lot of work,

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.820
like they have to really kind of get going

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:47.020
but then once they're up
they can kind of just open their wings.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:47.980
Based on his knowledge,

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:51.360
Ed actually agreed with this kind of
flight pattern for dragons and wyverns

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:53.380
I guess if a dragon lived on top of a mountain

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:55.080
and was prepared to throw itself off

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.080
and get enough lift to be able to take off and fly,

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:58.960
yeah, it could happen.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:00.560
That would be one to see.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.740
Unfortunately the way things are going,

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:05.700
the Livingstone's fruit bat might soon be just as mythical

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.300
as the dragons and griffins we've been talking about in this video.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.260
It's critically endangered.

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:14.530
Ed works on stopping the species from going extinct.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.160
Yeah, it's a priority project for us on the mammal department.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.200
At the minute their numbers are about

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:23.520
1260 across about 20 to 30 roosts on the two islands.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:28.030
So they're incredibly low, and the biggest threat at this minute in time would be a one-off cyclone

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.460
that could just pretty much decimate the entire population.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.589
So having them here in captivity is

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:38.040
still a critical safety net for the population, but it also facilitates a massive amount of research.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.460
We've got really good partners out in the Comoros called Dahari.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:41.460
They're an organization

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:43.080
we helped establish back in 2013,

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:44.920
but they're a complete separate NGO.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:46.840
They are doing amazing community

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.120
agroforestry and agricultural work.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.360
Durrell and their partners in the field do truly fantastic

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.740
work saving species from extinction
and working with local populations.

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:58.520
I'll leave a link in the description

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:01.680
so you can learn more about their work with loads of different animals

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:03.900
and if you have any money going spare,

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.320
every single penny donated makes a real difference to species like the Livingstone's.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:10.600
So after all this analysis,

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:13.480
which mythical beasts could really fly?

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.420
The major problem with a lot of the species
shown in fantasy is the musculature.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.120
Pectoral muscles are needed to power wings.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:23.040
So any species shown to have both front legs and wings?

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:25.300
Doesn't really make any sense.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:26.980
From a typical flying pantheon

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:29.460
that pretty much just leaves wyverns.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:35.020
Although two other possible species which Turps and Mark didn't mention are cockatrices and harpies.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.760
While real-life biology indicates that harpies,
women with wings for arms, could exist,

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.020
they would need a much bigger wingspan than is normally depicted.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.740
Dragon wing creatures like cockatrices and wyverns

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.820
wouldn't be able to flap their wings fast enough to take flight.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.740
But if we allow for a little bit of magic or even just alternative biology

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:55.840
these creatures might fly in a similar way to condors,

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:59.480
more about soaring than flapping and with an assisted takeoff.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:03.700
But if you play Horde, your mount might just be possible in the real world.

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:05.140
At the end of the day though

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.700
does it matter that the creatures in World of Warcraft

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:09.800
and Dungeons &amp; Dragons are unphysical?

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.060
No, absolutely not.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:13.070
I'm not looking for the real world in D&amp;D.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.600
Sometimes the brilliance of fantasy

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.000
is that it's not real

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.100
and that it's completely different and it's

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:21.160
imaginative and you can go somewhere.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:23.160
The beauty of Dungeons &amp; Dragons,

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:27.220
and I tend to favor campaigns that we call high magic settings,

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:29.060
you can't get that stuff in the real world.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.700
Like we have enough of the real world in the rest of our media,

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:35.880
whereas in fantasy almost anything goes and I love that.

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:38.160
Personally, I want my fantasy to remain crazy.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.120
You know, that's the point of it.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.480
I've got other, you know normal stuff,

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.160
it's called the real world

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:47.080
and I try to spend as little time in it as possible,

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:48.560
thank you very much.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.980
As a scientist, I love to watch fantasy

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:55.340
that's grounded itself in reality and tries to make its magic feel real.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.020
But sometimes you don't want that.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:59.740
Sometimes you want the fantastical

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:03.240
and based on the number of stories throughout history and around the world,

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:06.740
perhaps there's something in all of us that just wants

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:09.440
an impossible gigantic flying lizard

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:11.680
that breathes fire to be real.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:13.360
Just not near me, please.

